𝗗𝗮𝘆 𝟱𝟳/𝟯𝟲𝟱 — 𝗗𝗲𝘃𝗢𝗽𝘀 𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗖𝗵𝗮𝗹𝗹𝗲𝗻𝗴𝗲

🔥 𝗞𝘂𝗯𝗲𝗿𝗻𝗲𝘁𝗲𝘀 𝗜𝗻𝗴𝗿𝗲𝘀𝘀 𝗶𝘀 𝗡𝗼𝘁 𝗪𝗼𝗿𝗸𝗶𝗻𝗴 — 𝗛𝗼𝘄 𝗪𝗼𝘂𝗹𝗱 𝗬𝗼𝘂 𝗧𝗿𝗼𝘂𝗯𝗹𝗲𝘀𝗵𝗼𝗼𝘁 𝗜𝘁?

𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗤𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻:

Your application is working inside the Kubernetes cluster, and the Service is healthy. However, users cannot access it through the Ingress URL. How would you troubleshoot this in production?

Here’s my practical approach 👇

🔹 𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟭: 𝗖𝗵𝗲𝗰𝗸 𝗜𝗻𝗴𝗿𝗲𝘀𝘀 𝗥𝗲𝘀𝗼𝘂𝗿𝗰𝗲

kubectl get ingress -n <namespace>
kubectl describe ingress <ingress-name> -n <namespace>

Verify the hostname, path, backend Service, backend port, and Events.

🔹 𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟮: 𝗖𝗵𝗲𝗰𝗸 𝗜𝗻𝗴𝗿𝗲𝘀𝘀 𝗖𝗼𝗻𝘁𝗿𝗼𝗹𝗹𝗲𝗿

kubectl get pods -A | grep -i ingress
kubectl get svc -A | grep -i ingress

Confirm that the Ingress Controller is running and its Service is reachable.

🔹 𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟯: 𝗥𝗲𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗖𝗼𝗻𝘁𝗿𝗼𝗹𝗹𝗲𝗿 𝗟𝗼𝗴𝘀

kubectl logs <controller-pod> -n <controller-namespace> --tail=100

Look for configuration errors, backend connection failures, and rejected Ingress rules.

🔹 𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟰: 𝗩𝗲𝗿𝗶𝗳𝘆 𝗕𝗮𝗰𝗸𝗲𝗻𝗱 𝗦𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗰𝗲

kubectl get svc -n <namespace>
kubectl get endpointslices -n <namespace>

Confirm that the Ingress backend points to the correct Service and port, and that the Service has healthy endpoints.

🔹 𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟱: 𝗖𝗵𝗲𝗰𝗸 𝗗𝗡𝗦 𝗮𝗻𝗱 𝗧𝗟𝗦

nslookup <your-domain>

Verify that DNS resolves to the correct Ingress endpoint.

If HTTPS is failing, inspect the TLS Secret and certificate configuration:

kubectl get secrets -n <namespace>
kubectl describe ingress <ingress-name> -n <namespace>

🔹 𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟲: 𝗧𝗲𝘀𝘁 𝗧𝗵𝗲 𝗥𝗼𝘂𝘁𝗲

curl -vk https://<your-domain>/<path>

Check the HTTP response code, hostname routing, path rules, and backend response.

💡 𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗟𝗲𝗮𝗿𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴:

Ingress is not just a URL configuration. It depends on the Ingress Controller, routing rules, DNS, TLS, and backend Service.

Always troubleshoot the complete traffic path:

Client → DNS → Load Balancer → Ingress Controller → Service → Pod.
